    "content": "God Hates Us All was tracked at The Warehouse Studio in Vancouver, Canada — the historic recording facility located inside a 100-year-old former courthouse that Bryan Adams acquired and rebuilt in the 1990s. The room's natural high ceilings and stone construction gave the drum tracking a sense of space and ambient weight that Matt Hyde leveraged through careful room-mic placement.\n\n**Kit Tuning:**\nThe Tama Starclassic Maple shells were tuned darker and lower than the classic 1980s Slayer sound. Hyde wanted the kit to sit in the same low-end zone as the down-tuned guitars — a more contemporary production aesthetic than the dry, mid-forward Rick Rubin approach of the Reign in Blood era. The toms ring with more sustain than on the classic records.\n\n**Double Bass Production:**\nEach 22x18 bass drum was recorded with a Shure Beta 91A inside the shell against the resonant head and a Shure Beta 52A at the port — a two-mic-per-kick technique that captures both the attack from the beater and the body from the shell. The pedals were tracked dry and processed in the mix with parallel compression to maintain consistency across the album's many double-bass sections.\n\n**Room Sound:**\nThe Warehouse's natural reverb is audible on the album, particularly during slower sections like the bridge of \"Disciple\" where the drums breathe more. Hyde used Coles 4038 ribbon mics for room ambience — a classic choice that smooths the high-end while capturing the room character.\n\n**Snare Processing:**\nThe steel snare was tracked with Shure SM57 top and Sennheiser MD441 bottom, then heavily compressed for the cutting crack that defines the album's snare sound. The bottom-mic phase was inverted and gated to control bleed.",
